1
00:00:07,159 --> 00:00:14,350
Please welcome AI researcher and founding member of OpenAI, Andrej Karpathy.

2
00:00:21,169 --> 00:00:31,900
Hi, everyone. I'm happy to be here to tell you about the state of GPT and more generally about the rapidly growing ecosystem of large language models.

3
00:00:31,901 --> 00:00:35,015
I would like to partition the talk into two parts.

4
00:00:35,016 --> 00:00:40,610
In the first part, I would like to tell you about how we train GPT Assistance, and then in the second part,

5
00:00:40,611 --> 00:00:46,390
we're going to take a look at how we can use these assistants effectively for your applications.

6
00:00:46,391 --> 00:00:53,030
First, let's take a look at the emerging recipe for how to train these assistants and keep in mind that this is all very new and still rapidly evolving,

7
00:00:53,031 --> 00:00:55,535
but so far, the recipe looks something like this.

8
00:00:55,536 --> 00:01:01,250
Now, this is a complicated slide, I'm going to go through it piece by piece, but roughly speaking,

9
00:01:01,251 --> 00:01:09,635
we have four major stages, pretraining, supervised finetuning, reward modeling, reinforcement learning, and they follow each other serially.

10
00:01:09,636 --> 00:01:14,530
Now, in each stage, we have a dataset that powers that stage.

11
00:01:14,531 --> 00:01:22,070
We have an algorithm that for our purposes will be a objective and over for training the neural network,

12
00:01:22,071 --> 00:01:25,675
and then we have a resulting model, and then there are some notes on the bottom.

13
00:01:25,676 --> 00:01:28,580
The first stage we're going to start with as the pretraining stage.

14
00:01:28,581 --> 00:01:36,365
Now, this stage is special in this diagram, and this diagram is not to scale because this stage is where all of the computational work basically happens.

15
00:01:36,366 --> 00:01:41,970
This is 99 percent of the training compute time and also flops.

16
00:01:41,971 --> 00:01:51,095
This is where we are dealing with Internet scale datasets with thousands of GPUs in the supercomputer and also months of training potentially.

17
00:01:51,096 --> 00:01:59,045
The other three stages are finetuning stages that are much more along the lines of small few number of GPUs and hours or days.

18
00:01:59,046 --> 00:02:03,050
Let's take a look at the pretraining stage to achieve a base model.

19
00:02:03,051 --> 00:02:07,490
First, we are going to gather a large amount of data.

20
00:02:07,491 --> 00:02:16,600
Here's an example of what we call a data mixture that comes from this paper that was released by Meta where they released this LLaMA based model.

21
00:02:16,601 --> 00:02:20,445
Now, you can see roughly the datasets that enter into these collections.

22
00:02:20,446 --> 00:02:27,260
We have CommonCrawl, which is a web scrape, C4, which is also CommonCrawl, and then some high quality datasets as well.

23
00:02:27,261 --> 00:02:31,325
For example, GitHub, Wikipedia, Books, Archives, Stock Exchange and so on.

24
00:02:31,326 --> 00:02:40,425
These are all mixed up together, and then they are sampled according to some given proportions, and that forms the training set for the GPT.

25
00:02:40,426 --> 00:02:46,670
Now before we can actually train on this data, we need to go through one more preprocessing step, and that is tokenization.

26
00:02:46,671 --> 00:02:57,080
This is basically a translation of the raw text that we scrape from the Internet into sequences of integers because that's the native representation over which GPTs function.

27
00:02:57,081 --> 00:03:05,750
Now, this is a lossless translation between pieces of texts and tokens and integers, and there are a number of algorithms for the stage.

28
00:03:05,751 --> 00:03:13,750
Typically, for example, you could use something like byte pair encoding, which iteratively merges text chunks and groups them into tokens.

29
00:03:13,751 --> 00:03:21,295
Here, I'm showing some example chunks of these tokens, and then this is the raw integer sequence that will actually feed into a transformer.

30
00:03:21,296 --> 00:03:28,160
Now, here I'm showing two examples for hybrid parameters that govern this stage.

31
00:03:28,161 --> 00:03:33,845
GPT-4, we did not release too much information about how it was trained and so on, I'm using GPT-3s numbers,

32
00:03:33,846 --> 00:03:37,595
but GPT-3 is of course a little bit old by now, about three years ago.

33
00:03:37,596 --> 00:03:40,530
But LLaMA is a fairly recent model from Meta.

34
00:03:40,531 --> 00:03:44,780
These are roughly the orders of magnitude that we're dealing with when we're doing pretraining.

35
00:03:44,781 --> 00:03:48,140
The vocabulary size is usually a couple 10,000 tokens.

36
00:03:48,141 --> 00:03:53,240
The context length is usually something like 2,000, 4,000, or nowadays even 100,000,

37
00:03:53,241 --> 00:04:01,280
and this governs the maximum number of integers that the GPT will look at when it's trying to predict the next integer in a sequence.

38
00:04:01,281 --> 00:04:06,140
You can see that roughly the number of parameters say, 65 billion for LLaMA.

39
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:11,320
Now, even though LLaMA has only 65B parameters compared to GPP-3s 175 billion parameters,

40
00:04:11,321 --> 00:04:17,870
LLaMA is a significantly more powerful model, and intuitively, that's because the model is trained for significantly longer.

41
00:04:17,871 --> 00:04:21,670
In this case, 1.4 trillion tokens, instead of 300 billion tokens.

42
00:04:21,671 --> 00:04:26,275
You shouldn't judge the power of a model by the number of parameters that it contains.

43
00:04:26,276 --> 00:04:34,310
Below, I'm showing some tables of rough hyperparameters that typically go into specifying the transformer neural network,

44
00:04:34,311 --> 00:04:41,155
the number of heads, the dimension size, number of layers, and so on, and on the bottom I'm showing some training hyperparameters.

45
00:04:41,156 --> 00:04:52,560
For example, to train the 65B model, Meta used 2,000 GPUs, roughly 21 days of training and a roughly several million dollars.

46
00:04:52,561 --> 00:04:57,930
That's the rough orders of magnitude that you should have in mind for the pre-training stage.

47
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:00,905
Now, when we're actually pre-training, what happens?

48
00:05:00,906 --> 00:05:06,125
Roughly speaking, we are going to take our tokens, and we're going to lay them out into data batches.

49
00:05:06,126 --> 00:05:10,820
We have these arrays that will feed into the transformer, and these arrays are B,

50
00:05:10,821 --> 00:05:17,970
the batch size and these are all independent examples stocked up in rows and B by T, T being the maximum context length.

51
00:05:17,971 --> 00:05:23,030
In my picture I only have 10 the context lengths, so this could be 2,000, 4,000, etc.

52
00:05:23,031 --> 00:05:24,860
These are extremely long rows.

53
00:05:24,861 --> 00:05:31,685
What we do is we take these documents, and we pack them into rows, and we delimit them with these special end of texts tokens,

54
00:05:31,686 --> 00:05:35,005
basically telling the transformer where a new document begins.

55
00:05:35,006 --> 00:05:41,545
Here, I have a few examples of documents and then I stretch them out into this input.

56
00:05:41,546 --> 00:05:46,115
Now, we're going to feed all of these numbers into transformer.

57
00:05:46,116 --> 00:05:52,490
Let me just focus on a single particular cell, but the same thing will happen at every cell in this diagram.

58
00:05:52,491 --> 00:05:54,365
Let's look at the green cell.

59
00:05:54,366 --> 00:06:00,235
The green cell is going to take a look at all of the tokens before it, so all of the tokens in yellow,

60
00:06:00,236 --> 00:06:05,105
and we're going to feed that entire context into the transforming neural network,

61
00:06:05,106 --> 00:06:10,175
and the transformer is going to try to predict the next token in a sequence, in this case in red.

62
00:06:10,176 --> 00:06:13,040
Now the transformer, I don't have too much time to, unfortunately,

63
00:06:13,041 --> 00:06:18,950
go into the full details of this neural network architecture is just a large blob of neural net stuff for our purposes,

64
00:06:18,951 --> 00:06:22,450
and it's got several, 10 billion parameters typically or something like that.

65
00:06:22,451 --> 00:06:28,930
Of course, as I tune these parameters, you're getting slightly different predicted distributions for every single one of these cells.

66
00:06:28,931 --> 00:06:34,370
For example, if our vocabulary size is 50,257 tokens,

67
00:06:34,371 --> 00:06:40,220
then we're going to have that many numbers because we need to specify a probability distribution for what comes next.

68
00:06:40,221 --> 00:06:42,775
Basically, we have a probability for whatever may follow.

69
00:06:42,776 --> 00:06:51,625
Now, in this specific example, for this specific cell, 513 will come next, and so we can use this as a source of supervision to update our transformers weights.

70
00:06:51,626 --> 00:06:56,615
We're applying this basically on every single cell in the parallel, and we keep swapping batches,

71
00:06:56,616 --> 00:07:02,065
and we're trying to get the transformer to make the correct predictions over what token comes next in a sequence.

72
00:07:02,066 --> 00:07:05,465
Let me show you more concretely what this looks like when you train one of these models.

73
00:07:05,466 --> 00:07:11,060
This is actually coming from the New York Times, and they trained a small GPT on Shakespeare.

74
00:07:11,061 --> 00:07:14,410
Here's a small snippet of Shakespeare, and they train their GPT on it.

75
00:07:14,411 --> 00:07:19,190
Now, in the beginning, at initialization, the GPT starts with completely random weights.

76
00:07:19,191 --> 00:07:21,965
You're getting completely random outputs as well.

77
00:07:21,966 --> 00:07:26,015
But over time, as you train the GPT longer and longer,

78
00:07:26,016 --> 00:07:32,870
you are getting more and more coherent and consistent samples from the model, and the way you sample from it, of course,

79
00:07:32,871 --> 00:07:42,185
is you predict what comes next, you sample from that distribution and you keep feeding that back into the process, and you can basically sample large sequences.

80
00:07:42,186 --> 00:07:48,230
By the end, you see that the transformer has learned about words and where to put spaces and where to put commas and so on.

81
00:07:48,231 --> 00:07:51,115
We're making more and more consistent predictions over time.

82
00:07:51,116 --> 00:07:54,770
These are the plots that you are looking at when you're doing model pretraining.

83
00:07:54,771 --> 00:07:58,510
Effectively, we're looking at the loss function over time as you train,

84
00:07:58,511 --> 00:08:06,870
and low loss means that our transformer is giving a higher probability to the next correct integer in the sequence.

85
00:08:06,871 --> 00:08:10,720
What are we going to do with model once we've trained it after a month?

86
00:08:10,721 --> 00:08:18,560
Well, the first thing that we noticed, we the field, is that these models basically in the process of language modeling,

87
00:08:18,561 --> 00:08:21,185
learn very powerful general representations,

88
00:08:21,186 --> 00:08:26,410
and it's possible to very efficiently fine tune them for any arbitrary downstream tasks you might be interested in.

89
00:08:26,411 --> 00:08:29,225
As an example, if you're interested in sentiment classification,

90
00:08:29,226 --> 00:08:38,150
the approach used to be that you collect a bunch of positives and negatives and then you train some NLP model for that, but the new approach is:

91
00:08:38,151 --> 00:08:44,735
ignore sentiment classification, go off and do large language model pretraining, train a large transformer,

92
00:08:44,736 --> 00:08:51,040
and then you may only have a few examples and you can very efficiently fine tune your model for that task.

93
00:08:51,041 --> 00:08:53,550
This works very well in practice.

94
00:08:53,551 --> 00:09:00,415
The reason for this is that basically the transformer is forced to multitask a huge amount of tasks in the language modeling task,

95
00:09:00,416 --> 00:09:09,120
because in terms of predicting the next token, it's forced to understand a lot about the structure of the text and all the different concepts therein.

96
00:09:09,350 --> 00:09:17,920
That was GPT-1. Now around the time of GPT-2, people noticed that actually even better than fine tuning, you can actually prompt these models very effectively.

97
00:09:17,921 --> 00:09:25,620
These are language models and they want to complete documents, you can actually trick them into performing tasks by arranging these fake documents.

98
00:09:25,621 --> 00:09:31,025
In this example, for example, we have some passage and then we like do QA, QA, QA.

99
00:09:31,026 --> 00:09:37,810
This is called Few-shot prompt, and then we do Q, and then as the transformer is tried to complete the document is actually answering our question.

100
00:09:37,811 --> 00:09:45,740
This is an example of prompt engineering based model, making it believe that it's imitating a document and getting it to perform a task.

101
00:09:45,741 --> 00:09:48,635
This kicked off, I think the era of, I would say,

102
00:09:48,636 --> 00:09:53,375
prompting over fine tuning and seeing that this actually can work extremely well on a lot of problems,

103
00:09:53,376 --> 00:09:56,730
even without training any neural networks, fine tuning or so on.

104
00:09:56,731 --> 00:10:02,525
Now since then, we've seen an entire evolutionary tree of base models that everyone has trained.

105
00:10:02,526 --> 00:10:05,120
Not all of these models are available.

106
00:10:05,121 --> 00:10:08,240
for example, the GPT-4 base model was never released.

107
00:10:08,241 --> 00:10:15,800
The GPT-4 model that you might be interacting with over API is not a base model, it's an assistant model, and we're going to cover how to get those in a bit.

108
00:10:15,801 --> 00:10:24,395
GPT-3 based model is available via the API under the name Devanshi and GPT-2 based model is available even as weights on our GitHub repo.

109
00:10:24,396 --> 00:10:32,610
But currently the best available base model probably is the LLaMA series from Meta, although it is not commercially licensed.

110
00:10:32,879 --> 00:10:36,745
Now, one thing to point out is base models are not assistants.

111
00:10:36,746 --> 00:10:43,000
They don't want to make answers to your questions, they want to complete documents.

112
00:10:43,001 --> 00:10:51,385
If you tell them to write a poem about the bread and cheese, it will answer questions with more questions, it's completing what it thinks is a document.

113
00:10:51,386 --> 00:10:57,535
However, you can prompt them in a specific way for base models that is more likely to work.

114
00:10:57,536 --> 00:11:02,440
As an example, here's a poem about bread and cheese, and in that case it will autocomplete correctly.

115
00:11:02,441 --> 00:11:06,220
You can even trick base models into being assistants.

116
00:11:06,221 --> 00:11:16,315
The way you would do this is you would create a specific few-shot prompt that makes it look like there's some document between the human and assistant and they're exchanging information.

117
00:11:16,316 --> 00:11:17,860
Then at the bottom,

118
00:11:17,861 --> 00:11:26,650
you put your query at the end and the base model will condition itself into being a helpful assistant and answer,

119
00:11:26,651 --> 00:11:30,790
but this is not very reliable and doesn't work super well in practice, although it can be done.

120
00:11:30,791 --> 00:11:37,105
Instead, we have a different path to make actual GPT assistants not base model document completers.

121
00:11:37,106 --> 00:11:39,520
That takes us into supervised finetuning.

122
00:11:39,521 --> 00:11:45,940
In the supervised finetuning stage, we are going to collect small but high quality data-sets, and in this case,

123
00:11:45,941 --> 00:11:52,780
we're going to ask human contractors to gather data of the form prompt and ideal response.

124
00:11:52,781 --> 00:11:56,545
We're going to collect lots of these typically tens of thousands or something like that.

125
00:11:56,546 --> 00:11:59,515
Then we're going to still do language modeling on this data.

126
00:11:59,516 --> 00:12:02,860
Nothing changed algorithmically, we're swapping out a training set.

127
00:12:02,861 --> 00:12:11,410
It used to be Internet documents, which has a high quantity local for basically Q8 prompt response data.

128
00:12:11,411 --> 00:12:13,885
That is low quantity, high quality.

129
00:12:13,886 --> 00:12:18,190
We will still do language modeling and then after training, we get an SFT model.

130
00:12:18,191 --> 00:12:22,810
You can actually deploy these models and they are actual assistants and they work to some extent.

131
00:12:22,811 --> 00:12:25,750
Let me show you what an example demonstration might look like.

132
00:12:25,751 --> 00:12:28,255
Here's something that a human contractor might come up with.

133
00:12:28,256 --> 00:12:34,330
Here's some random prompt. Can you write a short introduction about the relevance of the term monopsony or something like that?

134
00:12:34,331 --> 00:12:37,360
Then the contractor also writes out an ideal response.

135
00:12:37,361 --> 00:12:45,820
When they write out these responses, they are following extensive labeling documentations and they are being asked to be helpful, truthful, and harmless.

136
00:12:45,821 --> 00:12:50,680
These labeling instructions here, you probably can't read it, neither can I,

137
00:12:50,681 --> 00:12:55,645
but they're long and this is people following instructions and trying to complete these prompts.

138
00:12:55,646 --> 00:12:57,430
That's what the dataset looks like.

139
00:12:57,431 --> 00:12:59,950
You can train these models. This works to some extent.

140
00:12:59,951 --> 00:13:05,170
Now, you can actually continue the pipeline from here on, and go into RLHF,

141
00:13:05,171 --> 00:13:10,375
reinforcement learning from human feedback that consists of both reward modeling and reinforcement learning.

142
00:13:10,376 --> 00:13:16,460
Let me cover that and then I'll come back to why you may want to go through the extra steps and how that compares to SFT models.

143
00:13:16,461 --> 00:13:23,010
In the reward modeling step, what we're going to do is we're now going to shift our data collection to be of the form of comparisons.

144
00:13:23,011 --> 00:13:25,355
Here's an example of what our dataset will look like.

145
00:13:25,356 --> 00:13:35,380
I have the same identical prompt on the top, which is asking the assistant to write a program or a function that checks if a given string is a palindrome.

146
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:41,170
Then what we do is we take the SFT model which we've already trained and we create multiple completions.

147
00:13:41,171 --> 00:13:47,035
In this case, we have three completions that the model has created, and then we ask people to rank these completions.

148
00:13:47,036 --> 00:13:52,855
If you stare at this for a while, and by the way, these are very difficult things to do to compare some of these predictions.

149
00:13:52,856 --> 00:14:03,055
This can take people even hours for a single prompt completion pairs, but let's say we decided that one of these is much better than the others and so on. We rank them.

150
00:14:03,056 --> 00:14:10,240
Then we can follow that with something that looks very much like a binary classification on all the possible pairs between these completions.

151
00:14:10,241 --> 00:14:16,150
What we do now is, we lay out our prompt in rows, and the prompt is identical across all three rows here.

152
00:14:16,151 --> 00:14:19,210
It's all the same prompt, but the completion of this varies.

153
00:14:19,211 --> 00:14:21,865
The yellow tokens are coming from the SFT model.

154
00:14:21,866 --> 00:14:31,720
Then what we do is we append another special reward readout token at the end and we basically only supervise the transformer at this single green token.

155
00:14:31,721 --> 00:14:42,145
The transformer will predict some reward for how good that completion is for that prompt and basically it makes a guess about the quality of each completion.

156
00:14:42,146 --> 00:14:47,965
Then once it makes a guess for every one of them, we also have the ground truth which is telling us the ranking of them.

157
00:14:47,966 --> 00:14:52,270
We can actually enforce that some of these numbers should be much higher than others, and so on.

158
00:14:52,271 --> 00:14:58,480
We formulate this into a loss function and we train our model to make reward predictions that are consistent with the ground truth coming

159
00:14:58,481 --> 00:15:01,105
from the comparisons from all these contractors.

160
00:15:01,106 --> 00:15:02,785
That's how we train our reward model.

161
00:15:02,786 --> 00:15:06,610
That allows us to score how good a completion is for a prompt.

162
00:15:06,611 --> 00:15:13,345
Once we have a reward model, we can't deploy this because this is not very useful as an assistant by itself,

163
00:15:13,346 --> 00:15:16,915
but it's very useful for the reinforcement learning stage that follows now.

164
00:15:16,916 --> 00:15:22,900
Because we have a reward model, we can score the quality of any arbitrary completion for any given prompt.

165
00:15:22,901 --> 00:15:26,065
What we do during reinforcement learning is we basically get, again,

166
00:15:26,066 --> 00:15:32,275
a large collection of prompts and now we do reinforcement learning with respect to the reward model. Here's what that looks like.

167
00:15:32,276 --> 00:15:35,995
We take a single prompt, we lay it out in rows,

168
00:15:35,996 --> 00:15:43,435
and now we use basically the model we'd like to train which was initialized at SFT model to create some completions in yellow,

169
00:15:43,436 --> 00:15:50,470
and then we append the reward token again and we read off the reward according to the reward model, which is now kept fixed.

170
00:15:50,471 --> 00:15:58,450
It doesn't change any more. Now the reward model tells us the quality of every single completion for all these prompts and so what we can do is we can now

171
00:15:58,451 --> 00:16:04,615
just basically apply the same language modeling loss function, but we're currently training on the yellow tokens,

172
00:16:04,616 --> 00:16:11,275
and we are weighing the language modeling objective by the rewards indicated by the reward model.

173
00:16:11,276 --> 00:16:13,165
As an example, in the first row,

174
00:16:13,166 --> 00:16:21,430
the reward model said that this is a fairly high-scoring completion and so all the tokens that we happen to sample on the first row are going to get

175
00:16:21,431 --> 00:16:25,075
reinforced and they're going to get higher probabilities for the future.

176
00:16:25,076 --> 00:16:29,935
Conversely, on the second row, the reward model really did not like this completion, -1.2.

177
00:16:29,936 --> 00:16:36,115
Therefore, every single token that we sampled in that second row is going to get a slightly higher probability for the future.

178
00:16:36,116 --> 00:16:43,090
We do this over and over on many prompts on many batches and basically, we get a policy that creates yellow tokens here.

179
00:16:43,091 --> 00:16:50,570
It's basically all the completions here will score high according to the reward model that we trained in the previous stage.

180
00:16:51,930 --> 00:16:55,540
That's what the RLHF pipeline is.

181
00:16:55,541 --> 00:16:58,930
Then at the end, you get a model that you could deploy.

182
00:16:58,931 --> 00:17:08,230
As an example, ChatGPT is an RLHF model, but some other models that you might come across for example, Vicuna-13B, and so on, these are SFT models.

183
00:17:08,231 --> 00:17:12,130
We have base models, SFT models, and RLHF models.

184
00:17:12,131 --> 00:17:14,500
That's the state of things there.

185
00:17:14,501 --> 00:17:16,915
Now why would you want to do RLHF?

186
00:17:16,916 --> 00:17:20,950
One answer that's not that exciting is that it works better.

187
00:17:20,951 --> 00:17:22,840
This comes from the instruct GPT paper.

188
00:17:22,841 --> 00:17:28,045
According to these experiments a while ago now, these PPO models are RLHF.

189
00:17:28,046 --> 00:17:33,775
We see that they are basically preferred in a lot of comparisons when we give them to humans.

190
00:17:33,776 --> 00:17:43,750
Humans prefer basically tokens that come from RLHF models compared to SFT models, compared to base model that is prompted to be an assistant. It just works better.

191
00:17:43,751 --> 00:17:47,335
But you might ask why does it work better?

192
00:17:47,336 --> 00:17:55,495
I don't think that there's a single amazing answer that the community has really agreed on, but I will offer one reason potentially.

193
00:17:55,496 --> 00:18:02,245
It has to do with the asymmetry between how easy computationally it is to compare versus generate.

194
00:18:02,246 --> 00:18:04,855
Let's take an example of generating a haiku.

195
00:18:04,856 --> 00:18:07,990
Suppose I ask a model to write a haiku about paper clips.

196
00:18:07,991 --> 00:18:14,245
If you're a contractor trying to train data, then imagine being a contractor collecting basically data for the SFT stage,

197
00:18:14,246 --> 00:18:16,840
how are you supposed to create a nice haiku for a paper clip?

198
00:18:16,841 --> 00:18:24,250
You might not be very good at that, but if I give you a few examples of haikus you might be able to appreciate some of these haikus a lot more than others.

199
00:18:24,251 --> 00:18:27,400
Judging which one of these is good is a much easier task.

200
00:18:27,401 --> 00:18:37,600
Basically, this asymmetry makes it so that comparisons are a better way to potentially leverage yourself as a human and your judgment to create a slightly better model.

201
00:18:37,601 --> 00:18:43,585
Now, RLHF models are not strictly an improvement on the base models in some cases.

202
00:18:43,586 --> 00:18:46,900
In particular, we'd notice for example that they lose some entropy.

203
00:18:46,901 --> 00:18:49,660
That means that they give more peaky results.

204
00:18:49,661 --> 00:18:55,945
They can output samples with lower variation than the base model.

205
00:18:55,946 --> 00:19:00,160
The base model has lots of entropy and will give lots of diverse outputs.

206
00:19:00,161 --> 00:19:09,805
For example, one place where I still prefer to use a base model is in the setup where you basically have

207
00:19:09,806 --> 00:19:13,735
n things and you want to generate more things like it.

208
00:19:13,736 --> 00:19:16,435
Here is an example that I just cooked up.

209
00:19:16,436 --> 00:19:18,790
I want to generate cool Pokemon names.

210
00:19:18,791 --> 00:19:24,580
I gave it seven Pokemon names and I asked the base model to complete the document and it gave me a lot more Pokemon names.

211
00:19:24,581 --> 00:19:27,010
These are fictitious. I tried to look them up.

212
00:19:27,011 --> 00:19:29,140
I don't believe they're actual Pokemons.

213
00:19:29,141 --> 00:19:33,370
This is the task that I think the base model would be good at because it still has lots of entropy.

214
00:19:33,371 --> 00:19:39,260
It'll give you lots of diverse cool more things that look like whatever you give it before.

215
00:19:41,820 --> 00:19:47,110
Having said all that, these are the assistant models that are probably available to you at this point.

216
00:19:47,111 --> 00:19:53,415
There was a team at Berkeley that ranked a lot of the available assistant models and give them basically Elo ratings.

217
00:19:53,416 --> 00:19:58,365
Currently, some of the best models, of course, are GPT-4, by far, I would say, followed by Claude,

218
00:19:58,366 --> 00:20:04,745
GPT-3.5, and then a number of models, some of these might be available as weights, like Vicuna, Koala, etc.

219
00:20:04,746 --> 00:20:14,210
The first three rows here are all RLHF models and all of the other models to my knowledge, are SFT models, I believe.

220
00:20:15,809 --> 00:20:19,240
That's how we train these models on the high level.

221
00:20:19,241 --> 00:20:26,080
Now I'm going to switch gears and let's look at how we can best apply the GPT assistant model to your problems.

222
00:20:26,081 --> 00:20:29,935
Now, I would like to work in setting of a concrete example.

223
00:20:29,936 --> 00:20:32,905
Let's work with a concrete example here.

224
00:20:32,906 --> 00:20:38,470
Let's say that you are working on an article or a blog post, and you're going to write this sentence at the end.

225
00:20:38,471 --> 00:20:44,725
"California's population is 53 times that of Alaska." So for some reason, you want to compare the populations of these two states.

226
00:20:44,726 --> 00:20:53,365
Think about the rich internal monologue and tool use and how much work actually goes computationally in your brain to generate this one final sentence.

227
00:20:53,366 --> 00:20:55,625
Here's maybe what that could look like in your brain.

228
00:20:55,626 --> 00:21:01,185
For this next step, let me blog on my blog, let me compare these two populations.

229
00:21:01,186 --> 00:21:05,010
First I'm going to obviously need to get both of these populations.

230
00:21:05,011 --> 00:21:12,835
Now, I know that I probably don't know these populations off the top of my head so I'm aware of what I know or don't know of my self-knowledge.

231
00:21:12,836 --> 00:21:19,825
I go, I do some tool use and I go to Wikipedia and I look up California's population and Alaska's population.

232
00:21:19,826 --> 00:21:26,905
Now, I know that I should divide the two, but again, I know that dividing 39.2 by 0.74 is very unlikely to succeed.

233
00:21:26,906 --> 00:21:30,340
That's not the thing that I can do in my head and so therefore,

234
00:21:30,341 --> 00:21:36,830
I'm going to rely on the calculator so I'm going to use a calculator, punch it in and see that the output is roughly 53.

235
00:21:36,831 --> 00:21:42,450
Then maybe I do some reflection and sanity checks in my brain so does 53 makes sense?

236
00:21:42,451 --> 00:21:47,930
Well, that's quite a large fraction, but then California is the most populous state, so maybe that looks okay.

237
00:21:47,931 --> 00:21:52,765
Then I have all the information I might need, and now I get to the creative portion of writing.

238
00:21:52,766 --> 00:21:58,480
I might start to write something like "California has 53x times greater" and then I think to myself,

239
00:21:58,481 --> 00:22:03,595
that's actually like really awkward phrasing so let me actually delete that and let me try again.

240
00:22:03,596 --> 00:22:06,295
As I'm writing, I have this separate process,

241
00:22:06,296 --> 00:22:15,625
almost inspecting what I'm writing and judging whether it looks good or not and then maybe I delete and maybe I reframe it, and then maybe I'm happy with what comes out.

242
00:22:15,626 --> 00:22:21,850
Basically long story short, a ton happens under the hood in terms of your internal monologue when you create sentences like this.

243
00:22:21,851 --> 00:22:26,765
But what does a sentence like this look like when we are training a GPT on it?

244
00:22:26,766 --> 00:22:30,585
From GPT's perspective, this is just a sequence of tokens.

245
00:22:30,586 --> 00:22:40,295
GPT, when it's reading or generating these tokens, it just goes chunk, chunk, chunk, chunk and each chunk is roughly the same amount of computational work for each token.

246
00:22:40,296 --> 00:22:47,965
These transformers are not very shallow networks they have about 80 layers of reasoning, but 80 is still not like too much.

247
00:22:47,966 --> 00:22:56,125
This transformer is going to do its best to imitate, but of course, the process here looks very different from the process that you took.

248
00:22:56,126 --> 00:23:00,340
In particular, in our final artifacts in the data sets that we create,

249
00:23:00,341 --> 00:23:07,810
and then eventually feed to LLMs, all that internal dialogue was completely stripped and unlike you,

250
00:23:07,811 --> 00:23:12,070
the GPT will look at every single token and spend the same amount of compute on every one of them.

251
00:23:12,071 --> 00:23:21,010
So, you can't expect it to do too much work per token and also in particular,

252
00:23:21,011 --> 00:23:26,080
basically these transformers are just like token simulators, they don't know what they don't know.

253
00:23:26,081 --> 00:23:27,820
They just imitate the next token.

254
00:23:27,821 --> 00:23:29,785
They don't know what they're good at or not good at.

255
00:23:29,786 --> 00:23:32,095
They just tried their best to imitate the next token.

256
00:23:32,096 --> 00:23:34,090
They don't reflect in the loop.

257
00:23:34,091 --> 00:23:35,500
They don't sanity check anything.

258
00:23:35,501 --> 00:23:37,285
They don't correct their mistakes along the way.

259
00:23:37,286 --> 00:23:40,600
By default, they just are sample token sequences.

260
00:23:40,601 --> 00:23:45,340
They don't have separate inner monologue streams in their head right? They're evaluating what's happening.

261
00:23:45,341 --> 00:23:48,445
Now, they do have some cognitive advantages,

262
00:23:48,446 --> 00:23:57,070
I would say and that is that they do actually have a very large fact-based knowledge across a vast number of areas because they have, say, several, 10 billion parameters.

263
00:23:57,071 --> 00:23:59,905
That's a lot of storage for a lot of facts.

264
00:23:59,906 --> 00:24:04,705
They also, I think have a relatively large and perfect working memory.

265
00:24:04,706 --> 00:24:14,890
Whatever fits into the context window is immediately available to the transformer through its internal self attention mechanism and so it's perfect memory,

266
00:24:14,891 --> 00:24:23,695
but it's got a finite size, but the transformer has a very direct access to it and so it can a losslessly remember anything that is inside its context window.

267
00:24:23,696 --> 00:24:29,230
This is how I would compare those two and the reason I bring all of this up is because I think to a large extent,

268
00:24:29,231 --> 00:24:39,520
prompting is just making up for this cognitive difference between these two architectures like our brains here and LLM brains.

269
00:24:39,521 --> 00:24:41,680
You can look at it that way almost.

270
00:24:41,681 --> 00:24:45,310
Here's one thing that people found for example works pretty well in practice.

271
00:24:45,311 --> 00:24:52,360
Especially if your tasks require reasoning, you can't expect the transformer to do too much reasoning per token.

272
00:24:52,361 --> 00:24:56,050
You have to really spread out the reasoning across more and more tokens.

273
00:24:56,051 --> 00:25:00,970
For example, you can't give a transformer a very complicated question and expect it to get the answer in a single token.

274
00:25:00,971 --> 00:25:02,545
There's just not enough time for it.

275
00:25:02,546 --> 00:25:06,985
"These transformers need tokens to think," I like to say sometimes.

276
00:25:06,986 --> 00:25:08,875
This is some of the things that work well,

277
00:25:08,876 --> 00:25:17,170
you may for example have a few-shot prompt that shows the transformer that it should show its work when it's answering question and if you give a few examples,

278
00:25:17,171 --> 00:25:24,220
the transformer will imitate that template and it will just end up working out better in terms of its evaluation.

279
00:25:24,221 --> 00:25:29,275
Additionally, you can elicit this behavior from the transformer by saying, let things step-by-step.

280
00:25:29,276 --> 00:25:40,285
Because this conditions the transformer into showing its work and because it snaps into a mode of showing its work, is going to do less computational work per token.

281
00:25:40,286 --> 00:25:46,015
It's more likely to succeed as a result because it's making slower reasoning over time.

282
00:25:46,016 --> 00:25:49,465
Here's another example, this one is called self-consistency.

283
00:25:49,466 --> 00:26:00,760
We saw that we had the ability to start writing and then if it didn't work out, I can try again and I can try multiple times and maybe select the one that worked best.

284
00:26:00,761 --> 00:26:03,790
In these approaches, you may sample not just once,

285
00:26:03,791 --> 00:26:11,965
but you may sample multiple times and then have some process for finding the ones that are good and then keeping just those samples or doing a majority vote or something like that.

286
00:26:11,966 --> 00:26:16,930
Basically these transformers in the process as they predict the next token, just like you,

287
00:26:16,931 --> 00:26:24,400
they can get unlucky and they could sample a not a very good token and they can go down like a blind alley in terms of reasoning.

288
00:26:24,401 --> 00:26:27,265
Unlike you, they cannot recover from that.

289
00:26:27,266 --> 00:26:34,195
They are stuck with every single token they sample and so they will continue the sequence, even if they know that this sequence is not going to work out.

290
00:26:34,196 --> 00:26:40,735
Give them the ability to look back, inspect or try to basically sample around it.

291
00:26:40,736 --> 00:26:47,335
Here's one technique also, it turns out that actually LLMs, they know when they've screwed up,

292
00:26:47,336 --> 00:26:55,915
so as an example, say you ask the model to generate a poem that does not rhyme and it might give you a poem, but it actually rhymes.

293
00:26:55,916 --> 00:27:01,120
But it turns out that especially for the bigger models like GPT-4, you can just ask it "did you meet the assignment?"

294
00:27:01,121 --> 00:27:04,825
Actually GPT-4 knows very well that it did not meet the assignment.

295
00:27:04,826 --> 00:27:07,240
It just got unlucky in its sampling.

296
00:27:07,241 --> 00:27:10,780
It will tell you, "No, I didn't actually meet the assignment here. Let me try again."

297
00:27:10,781 --> 00:27:17,980
But without you prompting it it doesn't know to revisit and so on.

298
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:23,050
You have to make up for that in your prompts, and you have to get it to check, if you don't ask it to check,

299
00:27:23,051 --> 00:27:26,330
its not going to check by itself it's just a token simulator.

300
00:27:28,169 --> 00:27:34,630
I think more generally, a lot of these techniques fall into the bucket of what I would say recreating our System 2.

301
00:27:34,631 --> 00:27:37,885
You might be familiar with the System 1 and System 2 thinking for humans.

302
00:27:37,886 --> 00:27:43,615
System 1 is a fast automatic process and I think corresponds to an LLM just sampling tokens.

303
00:27:43,616 --> 00:27:49,255
System 2 is the slower deliberate planning part of your brain.

304
00:27:49,256 --> 00:27:56,030
This is a paper actually from just last week because this space is pretty quickly evolving, it's called Tree of Thought.

305
00:27:56,340 --> 00:28:04,450
The authors of this paper proposed maintaining multiple completions for any given prompt and then they are also scoring them along

306
00:28:04,451 --> 00:28:08,380
the way and keeping the ones that are going well if that makes sense.

307
00:28:08,381 --> 00:28:19,540
A lot of people are really playing around with prompt engineering to basically bring back some of these abilities that we have in our brain for LLMs.

308
00:28:19,541 --> 00:28:22,780
Now, one thing I would like to note here is that this is not just a prompt.

309
00:28:22,781 --> 00:28:30,820
This is actually prompts that are together used with some Python Glue code because you actually have to maintain multiple prompts and you also have to do

310
00:28:30,821 --> 00:28:35,380
some tree search algorithm here to figure out which prompts to expand, etc.

311
00:28:35,381 --> 00:28:42,035
It's a symbiosis of Python Glue code and individual prompts that are called in a while loop or in a bigger algorithm.

312
00:28:42,036 --> 00:28:44,985
I also think there's a really cool parallel here to AlphaGo.

313
00:28:44,986 --> 00:28:52,385
AlphaGo has a policy for placing the next stone when it plays go, and its policy was trained originally by imitating humans.

314
00:28:52,386 --> 00:28:56,590
But in addition to this policy, it also does Monte Carlo Tree Search.

315
00:28:56,591 --> 00:29:01,975
Basically, it will play out a number of possibilities in its head and evaluate all of them and only keep the ones that work well.

316
00:29:01,976 --> 00:29:08,180
I think this is an equivalent of AlphaGo but for text if that makes sense.

317
00:29:08,190 --> 00:29:10,345
Just like Tree of Thought,

318
00:29:10,346 --> 00:29:17,455
I think more generally people are starting to really explore more general techniques of not just the simple question-answer prompts,

319
00:29:17,456 --> 00:29:22,060
but something that looks a lot more like Python Glue code stringing together many prompts.

320
00:29:22,061 --> 00:29:32,455
On the right, I have an example from this paper called React where they structure the answer to a prompt as a sequence of thought-action-observation,

321
00:29:32,456 --> 00:29:38,425
thought-action-observation, and it's a full rollout and a thinking process to answer the query.

322
00:29:38,426 --> 00:29:42,125
In these actions, the model is also allowed to tool use.

323
00:29:42,126 --> 00:29:45,390
On the left, I have an example of AutoGPT.

324
00:29:45,391 --> 00:29:55,140
Now AutoGPT by the way is a project that I think got a lot of hype recently, but I think I still find it inspirationally interesting.

325
00:29:55,141 --> 00:30:02,125
It's a project that allows an LLM to keep the task list and continue to recursively break down tasks.

326
00:30:02,126 --> 00:30:07,105
I don't think this currently works very well and I would not advise people to use it in practical applications.

327
00:30:07,106 --> 00:30:12,290
I just think it's something to generally take inspiration from in terms of where this is going, I think over time.

328
00:30:12,540 --> 00:30:16,165
That's like giving our model System 2 thinking.

329
00:30:16,166 --> 00:30:23,215
The next thing I find interesting is, this following serve I would say almost psychological quirk of LLMs,

330
00:30:23,216 --> 00:30:28,105
is that LLMs don't want to succeed, they want to imitate.

331
00:30:28,106 --> 00:30:31,105
You want to succeed, and you should ask for it.

332
00:30:31,106 --> 00:30:35,155
What I mean by that is, when transformers are trained,

333
00:30:35,156 --> 00:30:41,500
they have training sets and there can be an entire spectrum of performance qualities in their training data.

334
00:30:41,501 --> 00:30:45,415
For example, there could be some kind of a prompt for some physics question or something like that,

335
00:30:45,416 --> 00:30:50,950
and there could be a student's solution that is completely wrong but there can also be an expert answer that is extremely right.

336
00:30:50,951 --> 00:30:57,955
Transformers can't tell the difference between low, they know about low-quality solutions and high-quality solutions,

337
00:30:57,956 --> 00:31:02,425
but by default, they want to imitate all of it because they're just trained on language modeling.

338
00:31:02,426 --> 00:31:06,115
At test time, you actually have to ask for a good performance.

339
00:31:06,116 --> 00:31:10,885
In this example in this paper, they tried various prompts.

340
00:31:10,886 --> 00:31:15,655
Let's think step-by-step was very powerful because it spread out the reasoning over many tokens.

341
00:31:15,656 --> 00:31:20,905
But what worked even better is, let's work this out in a step-by-step way to be sure we have the right answer.

342
00:31:20,906 --> 00:31:23,710
It's like conditioning on getting the right answer,

343
00:31:23,711 --> 00:31:33,115
and this actually makes the transformer work better because the transformer doesn't have to now hedge its probability mass on low-quality solutions, as ridiculous as that sounds.

344
00:31:33,116 --> 00:31:37,225
Basically, feel free to ask for a strong solution.

345
00:31:37,226 --> 00:31:39,760
Say something like, you are a leading expert on this topic.

346
00:31:39,761 --> 00:31:41,935
Pretend you have IQ 120, etc.

347
00:31:41,936 --> 00:31:48,295
But don't try to ask for too much IQ because if you ask for IQ 400, you might be out of data distribution,

348
00:31:48,296 --> 00:31:56,980
or even worse, you could be in data distribution for something like sci-fi stuff and it will start to take on some sci-fi, or like roleplaying or something like that.

349
00:31:56,981 --> 00:31:59,485
You have to find the right amount of IQ.

350
00:31:59,486 --> 00:32:02,515
I think it's got some U-shaped curve there.

351
00:32:02,516 --> 00:32:12,025
Next up, as we saw when we are trying to solve problems, we know what we are good at and what we're not good at, and we lean on tools computationally.

352
00:32:12,026 --> 00:32:15,310
You want to do the same potentially with your LLMs.

353
00:32:15,311 --> 00:32:20,995
In particular, we may want to give them calculators, code interpreters,

354
00:32:20,996 --> 00:32:27,075
and so on, the ability to do search, and there's a lot of techniques for doing that.

355
00:32:27,076 --> 00:32:32,940
One thing to keep in mind, again, is that these transformers by default may not know what they don't know.

356
00:32:32,941 --> 00:32:37,580
You may even want to tell the transformer in a prompt you are not very good at mental arithmetic.

357
00:32:37,581 --> 00:32:42,490
Whenever you need to do very large number addition, multiplication, or whatever, instead, use this calculator.

358
00:32:42,491 --> 00:32:46,265
Here's how you use the calculator, you use this token combination, etc.

359
00:32:46,266 --> 00:32:54,610
You have to actually spell it out because the model by default doesn't know what it's good at or not good at, necessarily, just like you and I might be.

360
00:32:54,660 --> 00:33:02,215
Next up, I think something that is very interesting is we went from a world that was retrieval only all the way,

361
00:33:02,216 --> 00:33:06,115
the pendulum has swung to the other extreme where its memory only in LLMs.

362
00:33:06,116 --> 00:33:12,910
But actually, there's this entire space in-between of these retrieval-augmented models and this works extremely well in practice.

363
00:33:12,911 --> 00:33:17,020
As I mentioned, the context window of a transformer is its working memory.

364
00:33:17,021 --> 00:33:26,335
If you can load the working memory with any information that is relevant to the task, the model will work extremely well because it can immediately access all that memory.

365
00:33:26,336 --> 00:33:32,980
I think a lot of people are really interested in basically retrieval-augment degeneration.

366
00:33:32,981 --> 00:33:38,860
On the bottom, I have an example of LlamaIndex which is one data connector to lots of different types of data.

367
00:33:38,861 --> 00:33:44,200
You can index all of that data and you can make it accessible to LLMs.

368
00:33:44,201 --> 00:33:50,500
The emerging recipe there is you take relevant documents, you split them up into chunks, you embed all of them,

369
00:33:50,501 --> 00:33:53,320
and you basically get embedding vectors that represent that data.

370
00:33:53,321 --> 00:33:56,215
You store that in the vector store and then at test time,

371
00:33:56,216 --> 00:34:04,060
you make some kind of a query to your vector store and you fetch chunks that might be relevant to your task and you stuff them into the prompt and then you generate.

372
00:34:04,061 --> 00:34:06,385
This can work quite well in practice.

373
00:34:06,386 --> 00:34:09,355
This is, I think, similar to when you and I solve problems.

374
00:34:09,356 --> 00:34:17,830
You can do everything from your memory and transformers have very large and extensive memory, but also it really helps to reference some primary documents.

375
00:34:17,831 --> 00:34:21,025
Whenever you find yourself going back to a textbook to find something,

376
00:34:21,026 --> 00:34:27,610
or whenever you find yourself going back to documentation of the library to look something up, transformers definitely want to do that too.

377
00:34:27,611 --> 00:34:33,280
You have some memory over how some documentation of the library works but it's much better to look it up.

378
00:34:33,281 --> 00:34:35,840
The same applies here.

379
00:34:35,940 --> 00:34:39,685
Next, I wanted to briefly talk about constraint prompting.

380
00:34:39,686 --> 00:34:41,635
I also find this very interesting.

381
00:34:41,636 --> 00:34:50,335
This is basically techniques for forcing a certain template in the outputs of LLMs.

382
00:34:50,336 --> 00:34:53,455
Guidance is one example from Microsoft actually.

383
00:34:53,456 --> 00:34:57,805
Here we are enforcing that the output from the LLM will be JSON.

384
00:34:57,806 --> 00:35:04,630
This will actually guarantee that the output will take on this form because they go in and they mess with the probabilities of all the different tokens that

385
00:35:04,631 --> 00:35:09,940
come out of the transformer and they clamp those tokens and then the transformer is only filling in the blanks here,

386
00:35:09,941 --> 00:35:13,435
and then you can enforce additional restrictions on what could go into those blanks.

387
00:35:13,436 --> 00:35:18,410
This might be really helpful, and I think this constraint sampling is also extremely interesting.

388
00:35:19,219 --> 00:35:22,320
I also want to say a few words about fine tuning.

389
00:35:22,321 --> 00:35:29,415
It is the case that you can get really far with prompt engineering, but it's also possible to think about fine tuning your models.

390
00:35:29,416 --> 00:35:33,960
Now, fine tuning models means that you are actually going to change the weights of the model.

391
00:35:33,961 --> 00:35:43,050
It is becoming a lot more accessible to do this in practice, and that's because of a number of techniques that have been developed and have libraries for very recently.

392
00:35:43,051 --> 00:35:51,015
So for example parameter efficient fine tuning techniques like Laura, make sure that you're only training small, sparse pieces of your model.

393
00:35:51,016 --> 00:35:58,350
So most of the model is kept clamped at the base model and some pieces of it are allowed to change and this still works pretty well empirically and makes

394
00:35:58,351 --> 00:36:02,220
it much cheaper to tune only small pieces of your model.

395
00:36:02,221 --> 00:36:05,700
It also means that because most of your model is clamped,

396
00:36:05,701 --> 00:36:12,180
you can use very low precision inference for computing those parts because you are not going to be updated by gradient descent and so that

397
00:36:12,181 --> 00:36:13,965
makes everything a lot more efficient as well.

398
00:36:13,966 --> 00:36:17,460
And in addition, we have a number of open source, high-quality base models.

399
00:36:17,461 --> 00:36:23,520
Currently, as I mentioned, I think LLaMa is quite nice, although it is not commercially licensed, I believe right now.

400
00:36:23,521 --> 00:36:29,445
Some things to keep in mind is that basically fine tuning is a lot more technically involved.

401
00:36:29,446 --> 00:36:32,670
It requires a lot more, I think, technical expertise to do right.

402
00:36:32,671 --> 00:36:38,385
It requires human data contractors for datasets and/or synthetic data pipelines that can be pretty complicated.

403
00:36:38,386 --> 00:36:41,685
This will definitely slow down your iteration cycle by a lot,

404
00:36:41,686 --> 00:36:48,240
and I would say on a high level SFT is achievable because you're continuing the language modeling task.

405
00:36:48,241 --> 00:36:55,740
It's relatively straightforward, but RLHF, I would say is very much research territory and is even much harder to get to work,

406
00:36:55,741 --> 00:37:00,570
and so I would probably not advise that someone just tries to roll their own RLHF of implementation.

407
00:37:00,571 --> 00:37:06,000
These things are pretty unstable, very difficult to train, not something that is, I think, very beginner friendly right now,

408
00:37:06,001 --> 00:37:11,280
and it's also potentially likely also to change pretty rapidly still.

409
00:37:11,281 --> 00:37:15,435
So I think these are my default recommendations right now.

410
00:37:15,436 --> 00:37:18,315
I would break up your task into two major parts.

411
00:37:18,316 --> 00:37:23,985
Number 1, achieve your top performance, and Number 2, optimize your performance in that order.

412
00:37:23,986 --> 00:37:27,555
Number 1, the best performance will currently come from GPT-4 model.

413
00:37:27,556 --> 00:37:29,655
It is the most capable of all by far.

414
00:37:29,656 --> 00:37:31,950
Use prompts that are very detailed.

415
00:37:31,951 --> 00:37:36,240
They have lots of task content, relevant information and instructions.

416
00:37:36,241 --> 00:37:40,650
Think along the lines of what would you tell a task contractor if they can't email you back,

417
00:37:40,651 --> 00:37:46,140
but then also keep in mind that a task contractor is a human and they have inner monologue and they're very clever, etc.

418
00:37:46,141 --> 00:37:48,525
LLMs do not possess those qualities.

419
00:37:48,526 --> 00:37:54,930
So make sure to think through the psychology of the LLM almost and cater prompts to that.

420
00:37:54,931 --> 00:37:59,610
Retrieve and add any relevant context and information to these prompts.

421
00:37:59,611 --> 00:38:02,970
Basically refer to a lot of the prompt engineering techniques.

422
00:38:02,971 --> 00:38:11,580
Some of them I've highlighted in the slides above, but also this is a very large space and I would just advise you to look for prompt engineering techniques online.

423
00:38:11,581 --> 00:38:13,275
There's a lot to cover there.

424
00:38:13,276 --> 00:38:15,630
Experiment with few-shot examples.

425
00:38:15,631 --> 00:38:19,740
What this refers to is, you don't just want to tell, you want to show whenever it's possible.

426
00:38:19,741 --> 00:38:25,140
So give it examples of everything that helps it really understand what you mean if you can.

427
00:38:25,141 --> 00:38:30,420
Experiment with tools and plug-ins to offload tasks that are difficult for LLMs natively,

428
00:38:30,421 --> 00:38:33,450
and then think about not just a single prompt and answer,

429
00:38:33,451 --> 00:38:40,125
think about potential chains and reflection and how you glue them together and how you can potentially make multiple samples and so on.

430
00:38:40,126 --> 00:38:45,735
Finally, if you think you've squeezed out prompt engineering, which I think you should stick with for a while,

431
00:38:45,736 --> 00:38:51,345
look at some potentially fine tuning a model to your application,

432
00:38:51,346 --> 00:38:58,305
but expect this to be a lot more slower in the vault and then there's an expert fragile research zone here and I would say that is RLHF,

433
00:38:58,306 --> 00:39:02,220
which currently does work a bit better than SFT if you can get it to work.

434
00:39:02,221 --> 00:39:05,040
But again, this is pretty involved, I would say.

435
00:39:05,041 --> 00:39:11,950
And to optimize your costs, try to explore lower capacity models or shorter prompts and so on.

436
00:39:12,230 --> 00:39:18,675
I also wanted to say a few words about the use cases in which I think LLMs are currently well suited for.

437
00:39:18,676 --> 00:39:26,175
In particular, note that there's a large number of limitations to LLMs today, and so I would keep that definitely in mind for all of your applications.

438
00:39:26,176 --> 00:39:28,575
Models, and this by the way could be an entire talk.

439
00:39:28,576 --> 00:39:30,570
So I don't have time to cover it in full detail.

440
00:39:30,571 --> 00:39:35,550
Models may be biased, they may fabricate, hallucinate information, they may have reasoning errors,

441
00:39:35,551 --> 00:39:43,755
they may struggle in entire classes of applications, they have knowledge cut-offs, so they might not know any information above, say, September, 2021.

442
00:39:43,756 --> 00:39:48,795
They are susceptible to a large range of attacks which are coming out on Twitter daily,

443
00:39:48,796 --> 00:39:52,755
including prompt injection, jailbreak attacks, data poisoning attacks and so on.

444
00:39:52,756 --> 00:39:57,630
So my recommendation right now is use LLMs in low-stakes applications.

445
00:39:57,631 --> 00:40:00,135
Combine them always with human oversight.

446
00:40:00,136 --> 00:40:07,770
Use them as a source of inspiration and suggestions and think co-pilots, instead of completely autonomous agents that are just like performing a task somewhere.

447
00:40:07,771 --> 00:40:11,380
It's just not clear that the models are there right now.

448
00:40:11,470 --> 00:40:15,125
So I wanted to close by saying that GPT-4 is an amazing artifact.

449
00:40:15,126 --> 00:40:18,320
I'm very thankful that it exists, and it's beautiful.

450
00:40:18,321 --> 00:40:20,360
It has a ton of knowledge across so many areas.

451
00:40:20,361 --> 00:40:22,975
It can do math, code and so on.

452
00:40:22,976 --> 00:40:29,145
And in addition, there's this thriving ecosystem of everything else that is being built and incorporated into the ecosystem.

453
00:40:29,146 --> 00:40:35,685
Some of these things I've talked about, and all of this power is accessible at your fingertips.

454
